    pr_ref:  # 55cd
    description: "The `compact` subcommand produces a budget-limited projection of the behavior map, selecting a subset of nodes and edges via centrality heuristics. The top-level `nodes` and `edges` in the compact output are internally consistent — every edge endpoint appears in the node set. However, the `features` array (named slices of the graph for specific entry points) carries the original full-graph `node_ids` and `edge_ids` references unchanged, even when the referenced nodes and edges have been pruned. At small budget sizes, the great majority of feature references point at content that is not present. The pattern persists at larger budgets — at 10× the default budget, the same fraction of feature references still dangle — indicating the issue is structural rather than size-dependent.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) At default budget (`--max-symbols 100`), most feature references dangle.** Compact emits 100 nodes / 350 edges with all 16 features preserved unchanged. Reference check: `feature[0]` has `edge_ids=[4 IDs]` but only 1 of those edge IDs exists in the compact's 350 edges; `node_ids=[5 IDs]` but only 2 exist in the 100 nodes. `feature[7]` has `edge_ids=[19 IDs]` with only 2 present and `node_ids=[17 IDs]` with only 4 present. Features 8-14 each have `edge_ids=[]` and `node_ids=[1 ID]` with 0 of those node IDs present. Compact pass copies features wholesale from the source behavior map without filtering or rewriting referenced IDs.\n\n**(2) The dangling-rate is structural, not size-dependent.** Retested with `--max-symbols 1000` (10× the default): 1000 nodes / 8254 edges. Total: 10/70 feature `edge_ids` present, 20/71 feature `node_ids` present. Per-feature: `f0=1/4 edges, 2/5 nodes`; `f1=0/7, 1/6`; `f2=1/7, 2/6`; `f3=0/7, 1/6`; `f4=3/9, 4/8`. Dangling rate is ~85% even with 10× more symbols in the compact pool. Conclusion: increasing `--max-symbols` does NOT shrink the dangling-references count proportionally. The compact selection criteria (centrality coverage) don't co-vary with feature membership; feature-bearing node IDs are not being preferentially selected.\n\n**(3) Top-level `edges` / `nodes` self-consistency is 100% (350/350 endpoints present at default; 8254/8254 at 1k); only `features.edge_ids` / `features.node_ids` have dangling refs.** The compact's own primary graph is internally consistent; the dangling-references pattern is isolated to the features projection.\n\n### Invariant\n\nFeatures should be re-projected during compact construction — either dropping entries whose membership has been pruned below a threshold, expanding the node selection to preserve feature membership, or rewriting feature references to point only at retained nodes and edges. The features array's claim to describe slices of the compact's own graph requires that its referenced IDs exist in the compact output.\n\n### Scope\n\nEvery feature in every compact output. Dangling rate is consistently high (~85% even at 10× default budget) — the great majority of references dangle at typical budgets, and the pattern is structural.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`behavior_map.features[]` in `compact` output, specifically `features[].edge_ids` and `features[].node_ids` arrays.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers using compact output as a self-contained subset of the behavior map find that feature references cannot be followed. Compact output's claim to be a coherent reduction is not honored on the features section — downstream tooling that walks features by ID hits missing data on ~85% of references. The dangling pattern means compact is unusable as a self-contained corpus for any feature-walking analysis.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Re-project `features[].edge_ids` and `features[].node_ids` during compact construction: filter each feature's referenced IDs to the post-compaction node/edge set.\n- Alternatively, expand the compact's node selection to preserve feature membership — when a feature is included, include all the nodes and edges its IDs reference (treat features as additional inclusion seeds alongside centrality).\n- For features whose membership has been pruned below a threshold (e.g., <2 retained references), drop the feature entry entirely rather than emitting a near-empty reference list.\n- Add a property test that asserts every `features[].edge_ids` ID and every `features[].node_ids` ID is present in the compact's own `edges` / `nodes` arrays.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-zavin — which is deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 55cd
    fields:  # 55cd
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-857994"  # 55cd
      statement: "Features should be re-projected during compact construction — either dropping entries whose membership has been pruned below a threshold, expanding the node selection to preserve feature membership, or rewriting feature references to point only at retained nodes and edges."  # 55cd
      root_cause: "A subcommand that produces a compact projection of the behavior map selects a budget-limited set of nodes and edges via centrality heuristics. The top-level nodes and edges in the compact output are internally consistent — every edge endpoint appears in the node set. However, the features array (named slices of the graph for specific entry points) carries the original full-graph node-id and edge-id references unchanged, even when the referenced nodes and edges have been pruned from the compact output. At small budget sizes, the great majority of feature references point at content that is not present. The pattern persists at larger budgets — at ten times the default budget, the same fraction of feature references still dangle, indicating the issue is structural (centrality-based selection does not co-vary with feature membership) rather than size-dependent."  # 55cd
